Translate an electrical function into source connection and load requirements (@engineering-electrical-function: 0, is #chapter, #hephaestus-core "Nodal-equations-and-independent-power-balance.lingua#r_00C1FA6F14BA58242B0111E3A7") { r_000F14ED9998133B2218854532
**Begin with the service:** supply two resistive loads with a stated steady terminal-voltage range while limiting source current and dissipation in the supplying paths. This is a hypothetical engineering study. A predicted voltage is evidence about a model; it is not a component rating, a verified manufactured connection or proof of the load's thermal function. Revisit [Turn an intended use into requirements and observable evidence](Turn-an-intended-use-into-requirements-and-observable-evidence.lingua#r_0C3988C75ECA342B8BFC943458) and [Carry a response requirement through realization and inspection](Carry-a-response-requirement-through-realization-and-inspection.lingua#r_00B6F25CACF583F02249520A8B) for those distinctions.

The stipulated source is an ideal positive DC voltage E in series with positive source resistance R_s. A separate common connection resistance R_c leads to a feed node at potential v₀. Branch resistance r₁ connects the feed to load L₁, whose upper terminal is v₁; branch resistance r₂ similarly connects to load L₂ at v₂. Both loads return to the source's negative terminal through the declared ideal return conductor. All resistances are constant and positive. Return resistance, if physically important, must be included in a revised topology; setting a numerical reference never supplies a missing conductor.

Set the return potential to zero and name the source's accessible positive terminal V_t between R_s and R_c. E is the internal ideal-source voltage in this model. It is not inferred from chemical composition or treated as a universal battery law. Terminal V_t can differ from E when current flows. The model omits storage, induction, active control, temperature dependence and changing loads; the present calculation is a steady DC state, not startup or fault behavior.

**Invented screening requirements:** each load voltage must lie in [8,10.5] V; source current must not exceed 3.4 A. Dissipation limits, in the order R_s,R_c,r₁,r₂, are (6,4,2,3) W. These numbers define a bounded analytical comparison, not real product capabilities or electrical-installation requirements. Actual component ratings and environmental/duty conditions would need independent primary documentation and verification.

In this study useful electrical power means dissipation in L₁ and L₂, consistent with an intended resistive-load service. Source/connection dissipation and instrument dissipation are excluded from that numerator. Another intended function may require a different useful-output definition. Even here useful electrical power is not a validated heating rate or temperature field.

**Work:** draw every conductor, node and resistor with the named terminals; add reference and current arrows. Attach each requirement to an observable and a model boundary. Separate nominal parameters, permitted ranges, physical evidence and quantities yet to be measured. Explain how the question changes if a load becomes regulated constant power or the source imposes a current limit. [State the physical assumptions behind a lumped circuit](../../physics/core/State-the-physical-assumptions-behind-a-lumped-circuit.lingua#r_001A7088BC509FE999669C908D) and [Circuit approximations, sources and power](../../physics/electromagnetism/currents/Circuit-approximations-sources-resistance-and-power.lingua#r_0Y05GYZKN18BT1FY2GGQ6476W2) provide the shared physical preparation.

